<p>The United States transferred 22 crew members of the seized Iranian container ship MV Touska to Pakistan for repatriation, Pakistan&#x27;s Foreign Office said Monday, calling the move a U.S. confidence-building measure coordinated with Washington and Tehran. Iranian state media later said 15 of the sailors crossed into Iran through the Rimdan border terminal in Sistan-Baluchestan.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/9j_rKn04UUypVOOgsoLhudWuH4ptjM-F3o51f1FzZ0nnWQK1HgP4o0Sl3V16IY203ZLbViGS4zm_hhoH4ZJ7KDkgYPdpURfPTdOuaUcPUHml' alt='Crew members of the Iranian container vessel, M/V Touska, stand with their belongings after they were evacuated and handed over to Iranian authorities at the Gabd-Rimdan border crossing near Gwadar, Pakistan, May 4, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Seizure site: The Iranian-flagged Touska was boarded and seized by U.S. forces near Iran&#x27;s Chabahar port in the Gulf of Oman last month.</li><li>Warning claim: U.S. Central Command said the crew failed to comply with repeated warnings during a six-hour period before the vessel was seized.</li><li>Vessel track: Pakistan said the Touska would be moved back to Pakistani territorial waters for repairs before return to its owners, separate from the sailors&#x27; handover.</li><li>Iran&#x27;s objection: Tehran rejected the U.S. seizure as unlawful and a violation of international maritime law, demanding immediate release of the ship and crew.</li></ul><blockquote>Pakistan welcomes such confidence-building measures and will continue to facilitate dialogue and diplomacy while pursuing ongoing mediation efforts for regional peace and security. - Pakistan Foreign Office</blockquote><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/04/world/middleeast/iran-ship-crew-pakistan.html'>Pakistan Says It Helped Iranian Crew From Ship That U.S. Seized</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1997392/iranian-sailors-evacuated-to-pakistan-return-home-state-media